% Contested node: presumption for Q versus presumption-backed challenge.
% The .fspy form of tests/test_evaluate.py::TestContested.
%
% pArg argues P via pRule from a DELEGATED Q ("Q by default"); qAtt is a
% counterargument challenging Q, itself resting on a delegation.  Both
% sides of Q are presumption-backed, so the grounded labelling is UNDEC
% on (Q, term), (Q, context) and (P, term) - a contested node needs no
% propositional cycle (mutual rebut is a two-statement negation cycle;
% see the V-two correction in propositional-fragment-plan.org).
%
% The evaluation modes therefore diverge:
%   evaluate datt skeptical  -> OPEN  (the UNDEC attacked side is dropped)
%   evaluate datt credulous  -> VALUE (the UNDEC attacker is dropped; the
%                                      presumption !Q stands in the term)
lk.
declare P,Q:bool.
declare pRule : (Q->P).

% Argument for P with the Q antecedent taken by default.
argument pArg : (P).
cut (Q->P) rule.
axiom pRule.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom rule.
dixi.

% Challenge to Q, itself delegated.
counterargument qAtt : (Q).
by default.
dixi.

debate pro open datt : P.
pArg.
attack qAtt pArg.
cedat tempus.

graph datt.
label datt.
evaluate datt skeptical.
evaluate datt credulous.
